# Codex Agent — Essora Web SDK: Browser Validation + Real SaaS Evaluation

## Context
All 10 SDK features are implemented and unit tests pass (16/16).
What is NOT yet proven:
- Pixel drift under 4px during scroll
- Cursor animation smoothness at 60fps
- Real multi-step guide on an actual SaaS UI
- Screenshot + vision reasoning quality
- Target lock surviving route changes
- Onboarding checklist against a live backend

Your job is to write and run a full browser evaluation suite using
Playwright + CDP. No new features. Validation only.

Read the full SDK first before writing a single test.

---

## Step 0 — Read before you write

Read these files:
- src/core/ClickyClient.ts          ← public API, events
- src/core/GuideModeRuntime.ts      ← guide loop, events
- src/overlay/CursorAnimator.ts     ← bezier flight, timing
- src/overlay/CursorOverlay.ts      ← transient mode, fade
- src/overlay/TargetLock.ts         ← scroll/resize/route lock
- src/overlay/ElementHighlighter.ts ← highlight rect
- src/context/SemanticGraph.ts      ← graph build, stableId
- src/panel/OnboardingPanel.ts      ← checklist flow
- src/panel/CompanionPanel.ts       ← settings, waveform
- src/context/ScreenshotCapture.ts  ← getDisplayMedia path
- demo/main.ts                      ← demo app entry point
- demo/index.html                   ← demo page structure
- package.json                      ← test/build scripts

---

## Step 1 — Setup Playwright

Install Playwright if not present:
  npm install --save-dev playwright @playwright/test

Add to package.json scripts:
  "test:e2e": "playwright test",
  "test:e2e:headed": "playwright test --headed"

Create playwright.config.ts at repo root:
- baseURL: http://127.0.0.1:5173
- browser: chromium only
- headless: true
- slowMo: 0
- video: on-failure
- screenshot: on-failure
- trace: on-failure

---

## Step 2 — Evaluation harness fixtures

Create e2e/fixtures/sdk-fixture.ts

This fixture must:
1. Launch demo page at /demo/
2. Wait for SDK to initialize (listen for clicky:ready event on window)
3. Expose helpers:
   - startGuide(goal: string): triggers SDK guide via window.__clicky.startGuide()
   - waitForEvent(event: string, timeout?: number): returns event payload
   - getCursorPosition(): returns { x, y } of the cursor overlay element
     via getBoundingClientRect()
   - getHighlightRect(): returns DOMRect of the highlight element
   - getTargetRect(stableId: string): returns DOMRect of the target element
   - scrollPage(px: number): window.scrollBy(0, px)
   - triggerRouteChange(path: string): history.pushState({}, '', path)
   - getDiagnostics(): returns window.__clicky.getDiagnostics()

---

## Step 3 — Write evaluation tests

Create these test files in e2e/:

---

### e2e/01-cursor-animation.spec.ts
**Goal:** Prove bezier flight, rotation, pulse, fade, reduced-motion

Test 1 — cursor flies to target:
1. Call startGuide("click the submit button")
2. waitForEvent("cursor:pointing")
3. Record cursor position at t=0 (before flight)
4. Record cursor position at arrival
5. Assert: cursor moved (positions are different)
6. Assert: cursor is within 20px of the target element center

Test 2 — cursor fades after inactivity:
1. Complete a guide step
2. Wait for TTS complete event
3. Wait 1200ms
4. Get cursor overlay opacity via computed style
5. Assert: opacity === "0" or visibility === "hidden"

Test 3 — reduced-motion skips flight:
1. Inject CSS: @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { * {
   animation-duration: 0ms !important; transition-duration: 0ms !important; } }
   OR set via CDP: page.emulateMedia({ reducedMotion: 'reduce' })
2. startGuide("click the submit button")
3. waitForEvent("cursor:pointing")
4. Assert: cursor reached target in under 50ms (no flight delay)

Test 4 — arrival bob:
1. waitForEvent("cursor:pointing")
2. Wait 100ms
3. Take cursor Y position
4. Wait 700ms (half bob cycle ~600ms)
5. Take cursor Y again
6. Assert: Y positions differ by 2-6px (bob is happening)

---

### e2e/02-target-lock.spec.ts
**Goal:** Prove highlight stays on element during scroll, resize, route change

Test 1 — highlight follows element on scroll:
1. startGuide(goal that points to an element below the fold)
2. waitForEvent("cursor:pointing")
3. Record highlight rect: R1 = getHighlightRect()
4. Record target rect: T1 = getTargetRect(stableId)
5. scrollPage(300)
6. await requestAnimationFrame (page.evaluate(() => new Promise(r => requestAnimationFrame(r))))
7. Record highlight rect: R2 = getHighlightRect()
8. Record target rect: T2 = getTargetRect(stableId)
9. Assert: |R2.top - T2.top| < 4   ← drift under 4px
10. Assert: |R2.left - T2.left| < 4

Test 2 — highlight survives resize:
1. startGuide, waitForEvent("cursor:pointing")
2. page.setViewportSize({ width: 800, height: 600 })
3. Wait one RAF
4. Recheck highlight vs target rects
5. Assert drift < 4px

Test 3 — route change re-lock:
1. startGuide, waitForEvent("cursor:pointing"), note stableId from event
2. triggerRouteChange('/other-page')
3. Wait 2500ms
4. If target element exists on new page: assert highlight re-attached
5. If target does not exist: assert "cursor:target-lost" event fired

Test 4 — target-lost event on element removal:
1. startGuide pointing to an element
2. waitForEvent("cursor:pointing")
3. page.evaluate(() => document.querySelector('[data-target]').remove())
4. Wait 500ms
5. Assert "cursor:target-lost" event fired

---

### e2e/03-semantic-graph.spec.ts
**Goal:** Prove graph captures the right elements and stableId is deterministic

Test 1 — graph contains interactive elements:
1. page.evaluate(() => window.__clicky.getContext())
2. Parse semanticGraph from context
3. Assert: graph contains at least one button, one input, one link
4. Assert: every node has stableId, role, name, bounds
5. Assert: bounds.width > 0 and bounds.height > 0 for visible elements

Test 2 — stableId is deterministic:
1. Call getContext() twice without changing DOM
2. Compare semanticGraph arrays
3. Assert: every stableId in call 1 exists in call 2
4. Assert: no stableId changed between calls

Test 3 — redacted fields excluded:
1. page.evaluate(() => {
     const el = document.createElement('input')
     el.type = 'password'
     el.id = 'test-password'
     document.body.appendChild(el)
   })
2. Call getContext()
3. Assert: semanticGraph does not contain node with id 'test-password'

Test 4 — max 120 nodes:
1. page.evaluate(() => {
     for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
       const b = document.createElement('button')
       b.textContent = 'btn ' + i
       document.body.appendChild(b)
     }
   })
2. Call getContext()
3. Assert: semanticGraph.length <= 120

---

### e2e/04-guide-mode.spec.ts
**Goal:** Prove multi-step guide runs correctly on the demo app

Use the demo app's existing UI — do not mock the AI.
If AI calls fail in CI (no keys), skip with test.skip and note in output.

Test 1 — guide produces multiple steps:
1. startGuide("fill out the contact form")
2. waitForEvent("guide:plan-ready", { timeout: 10000 })
3. Assert: event payload has steps array with length >= 2
4. Assert: each step has stepId, instruction, targetHint

Test 2 — guide advances on stepCompleted:
1. startGuide("fill out the contact form")
2. waitForEvent("guide:step-active")
3. Note stepId: S1
4. page.evaluate(() => window.__clicky.stepCompleted())
5. waitForEvent("guide:step-active")
6. Note stepId: S2
7. Assert: S2 !== S1

Test 3 — guide points to a real element each step:
1. startGuide, waitForEvent("guide:step-active")
2. waitForEvent("cursor:pointing")
3. Get stableId from event
4. Assert: document.querySelector([data-clicky-id="<stableId>"]) exists

Test 4 — recovery fires after timeout:
1. startGuide("complete checkout")
2. waitForEvent("guide:step-active")
3. Do NOT call stepCompleted — wait 16000ms
4. waitForEvent("guide:recovering", { timeout: 17000 })
5. Assert: event fired (recovery attempted)

Test 5 — cancelGuide stops the loop:
1. startGuide("fill out the form")
2. waitForEvent("guide:step-active")
3. page.evaluate(() => window.__clicky.cancelGuide())
4. Wait 500ms
5. Assert: getDiagnostics().state === "idle"

---

### e2e/05-voice-waveform.spec.ts
**Goal:** Prove mic level events fire and silent detection works

Note: real mic is not available in headless CI. Use fake audio.

Setup: launch browser with fake audio:
  use: { launchOptions: { args: ['--use-fake-device-for-media-stream',
                                  '--use-fake-ui-for-media-stream'] } }

Test 1 — mic:level event fires during recording:
1. page.evaluate(() => window.__clicky.startListening())
2. Collect mic:level events for 500ms
3. page.evaluate(() => window.__clicky.stopListening())
4. Assert: at least 5 mic:level events received
5. Assert: all values are 0.0–1.0

Test 2 — mic:silent fires after 3s of silence:
1. Start listening with fake (silent) audio device
2. Wait 3500ms
3. Assert: mic:silent event fired

Test 3 — permission denied state:
1. Override getUserMedia to reject:
   page.evaluate(() => {
     navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia = () =>
       Promise.reject(new DOMException('denied', 'NotAllowedError'))
   })
2. page.evaluate(() => window.__clicky.startListening())
3. waitForEvent("mic:permission-denied")
4. Assert: event fired

---

### e2e/06-onboarding.spec.ts
**Goal:** Prove onboarding checklist runs and dismisses correctly

Test 1 — onboarding shows on first load:
1. Clear localStorage before page load
2. Load demo page
3. Wait for SDK ready
4. Assert: onboarding panel is visible in shadow DOM
5. Assert: panel contains checklist items (mic, worker, AI, TTS)

Test 2 — checklist items complete:
1. showOnboarding()
2. Wait 8000ms for all checks
3. Query checklist item states
4. Assert: worker health check passed (demo Worker must be running)
5. Note: mic/AI/TTS may fail in CI — assert they show a result, not hang

Test 3 — dismiss persists:
1. showOnboarding()
2. Click dismiss button
3. Reload page
4. Assert: onboarding panel does NOT show on reload

Test 4 — showOnboarding() reopens it:
1. Dismiss onboarding
2. page.evaluate(() => window.__clicky.showOnboarding())
3. Assert: panel visible again

---

### e2e/07-screenshot-vision.spec.ts
**Goal:** Prove screenshot capture attaches to AI request when enabled

Note: getDisplayMedia requires user gesture and cannot be auto-approved
in headless Chromium. Use CDP to grant display-capture permission and
fake screen stream.

Setup:
  const context = await browser.newContext({
    permissions: ['display-capture'],
  })
  args: ['--auto-select-desktop-capture-source=Entire screen',
         '--use-fake-device-for-media-stream',
         '--use-fake-ui-for-media-stream']

Test 1 — screenshot capture succeeds:
1. Set screenshotMode: "user-triggered" in config
2. page.evaluate(() => window.__clicky.triggerScreenshot())
3. waitForEvent("screenshot:captured", { timeout: 5000 })
4. Get event payload
5. Assert: payload.dataUrl starts with "data:image/jpeg"
6. Assert: payload.width <= 1280

Test 2 — screenshot attaches to next AI request:
1. Trigger screenshot
2. waitForEvent("screenshot:captured")
3. Intercept next /ai/chat Worker request via page.route()
4. startGuide("what do you see on my screen")
5. Assert: intercepted request body contains an image content block
6. Assert: image content block type === "base64" and mediaType === "image/jpeg"

Test 3 — screenshot off by default:
1. Do NOT set screenshotMode
2. Intercept /ai/chat requests
3. startGuide("help me find the search bar")
4. waitForEvent("guide:step-active")
5. Assert: intercepted request body does NOT contain an image content block

Test 4 — denied gracefully degrades:
1. Override getDisplayMedia to reject:
   page.evaluate(() => {
     navigator.mediaDevices.getDisplayMedia = () =>
       Promise.reject(new DOMException('denied', 'NotAllowedError'))
   })
2. page.evaluate(() => window.__clicky.triggerScreenshot())
3. waitForEvent("screenshot:denied")
4. Assert: event fired, no unhandled error in console

